WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.620
Each port solved one problem alone.

2
00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.080
Production needs all of them at once.

3
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.690
So now you build one system, and then you try to break it.

4
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:11.640
This port has two parts: the fleet on your laptop,

5
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.615
and the same fleet on Google Cloud.

6
00:00:14.635 --> 00:00:17.375
First: one namespace with everything.

7
00:00:17.395 --> 00:00:19.563
Tugboat with probes and autoscaling.

8
00:00:19.583 --> 00:00:21.400
A database with its own disk.

9
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.235
And a scheduled job.

10
00:00:23.255 --> 00:00:27.695
Around them: a gateway, network policies, and least-privilege accounts.

11
00:00:27.715 --> 00:00:28.995
Then the chaos drills.

12
00:00:29.015 --> 00:00:29.958
Delete a pod.

13
00:00:29.978 --> 00:00:30.858
Drain a node.

14
00:00:30.878 --> 00:00:32.970
Send an intruder.

15
00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.880
Second: the same files, on a real cloud.

16
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.490
You push the image to a registry, and change two lines.

17
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.228
The manifests stay the same.

18
00:00:41.248 --> 00:00:43.450
That is the point of Kubernetes.

19
00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.312
Delete the cluster when you finish.

20
00:00:45.332 --> 00:00:48.925
A load balancer costs money every hour.

21
00:00:48.945 --> 00:00:51.085
Quick recap of the capstone.

22
00:00:51.105 --> 00:00:52.748
Every port in one namespace.

23
00:00:52.768 --> 00:00:54.035
Prove it with chaos.

24
00:00:54.055 --> 00:00:56.445
And the same files run anywhere.

25
00:00:56.465 --> 00:00:59.565
Now start the first lesson below.
